# Shear frame natural frequencies

A two storey shear frame has a lumped mass at each floor and a lateral stiffness per storey.
Free vibration needs K φ = ω² M φ. Multiplying by the inverse of M turns this into an ordinary
eigenvalue problem of the matrix M⁻¹K, which is not symmetric; `eigenvals` and `eigenvecs`
accept it as it is.

m_1: 30 t
m_2: 20 t
k_1: 40000 kN/m
k_2: 30000 kN/m

K: [k_1 + k_2, -k_2; -k_2, k_2]
M: [m_1, 0 t; 0 t, m_2]
MK: inverse(M)*K

## Frequencies

lambda: eigenvals(MK)
omega_1: sqrt(lambda.1)
omega_2: sqrt(lambda.2)
f_1: omega_1/(2*π) -> Hz
f_2: omega_2/(2*π) -> Hz
T_1: 1/f_1 -> s

## Mode shapes

Each row of `eigenvecs` is one mode, in the order of the frequencies. Scaled so the roof moves 1:

Phi: eigenvecs(MK)
phi_1: row(Phi, 1)/Phi.(1, 2)
phi_2: row(Phi, 2)/Phi.(2, 2)

The modes are orthogonal through the mass matrix, so this product is zero:

check: dot(phi_1, M*phi_2)
